<p>General contractor {% person "n-a-freeburg" %}N. A. Freeburg{% endperson %}, who was of Swedish descent, built the house in 1907 for his bride, {% person "esther-freeburg" %}Esther{% endperson %}, intending it as their permanent home. Freeburg and his partner, {% person "charles-kidd" %}Charles Kidd{% endperson %}, constructed several prominent Mandan buildings, including the H. R. Lyon home at 309 Fourth Avenue NW and the First National Bank Building.</p>

<p>The Freeburgs sold the house in 1910 to {% person "gustav-a-renden" %}Gustav A. Renden{% endperson %}, co-owner of the Mandan Roller Mill and a founder of the Mandan Electric Company.</p>

<p>H. A. Jones purchased it in 1940, followed by bank examiner Oren Baertsch in 1942 and Piggly Wiggly owner Bill Joersz in 1949.</p>

<p>Attorney Maurice LaGrave purchased the house in 1959. Gene and Nola Helm, owners of Helm Brothers Sand and Gravel, followed in 1967. Intending to make it their permanent home, the Helms undertook a major restoration. They added a room at the rear, a utility room, and a garage while retaining the original stairs, sliding doors, woodwork, pillars, full-wall bookshelves, windows, and distinctively finished stone foundation. The former carriage house at the rear of the property is now a separate residence next door.</p>

<p>Chuck and Janet Esser of Camas, Washington, purchased the home in 1990. Janet's maternal and paternal ancestors had immigrated to the Sims area a century earlier.</p>

<p>The house has a warm, light atmosphere, and its front porch has long been a favorite gathering place for friends and family.</p>
